
This thesis explored various approaches to Word Sense Induction within industrial technical and noisy data. 
Its two-level architecture first hypothesises that noun-based sentence embeddings - static and contextual - are 
more efficient than full-sentence versions, then evaluates four methods of leveraging these embeddings against 
two baselines. The general aim was to assess whether these solutions demonstrate robustness against the noise and 
highly technical vocabulary characteristic of industrial data. After optimizing them on open-source Wikipedia data, 
the study addressed the following questions: is a noun-only sentence representation more efficient than the full-sentence 
version? Which system performs best within each family? And can those systems also perform well on customer data? 
The results show that noun-based static sentence representations can outperform full-sentence embeddings, especially 
if constructed using an exponential decay method. The same remains unclear for noun-only contextual sentence embeddings. 
The transfer of the models optimized on the Wikipedia dataset to customer data failed, proving that the latter exhibits 
more noise and technicality than expected. Although limited by scarcity of customer data, this study highlights the 
potential of Word Sense Induction systems engineered to be robust against noise and technical vocabulary and creates 
ground for further research through its findings.
